\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}=\frac{1}{4}F_{\mu\nu}^{a}F^{a\,\mu\nu}-\lambda_{ab}
\Omega^{a}_{\mu}\Omega^{b\,\mu},
\end{equation}
where $\Omega^{\mu a}$ represents a non-abelian aether field, $\lambda_{ab}$ is a symmetric and positive-definite tensor, and $\lambda$ denotes its determinant. By employing an effective potential method, we derive the theory's finite-temperature Lagrangian. Furthermore, we investigate the temperature-dependent behavior of the aether field and the theory's vacuum state. Our findings indicate that, within this model, the vacuum remains stable at all temperatures, with the aether field acquiring a non-zero vacuum expectation value (VEV) at sufficiently low temperatures. Additionally, we demonstrate that the theory's vacuum state undergoes a phase transition at a specific critical temperature. Finally, we establish that the theory supports a massive photon, with the photon's mass increasing as the temperature rises. These results suggest that this non-abelian aether field model offers a viable alternative to standard electrodynamics at finite temperatures. Finally, we discuss the potential implications of these findings for cosmology and astrophysics. The calculations were performed using the Mathematica package xAct. Notably, our approach differs from the standard aether field theory, which relies on a non-abelian derivative-coupled aether field. In contrast, we utilize an effective aether field, a concept that has not been previously explored. Our findings suggest that this effective aether field model is more promising for cosmological and astrophysical applications than models utilizing the standard aether field. This work was conducted within the context of the International Centre for Theoretical Physics (ICTP) Summer School on Gravity, Cosmology, and Astrophysics 2021. We extend our gratitude to the ICTP for the opportunity to participate in this event. The research of A. A. and A. A. A. was partially funded by the Russian Science Foundation (RSF) under grant 20-12-00289. A. A. A.'s research was also supported by the Ministry

